# NGN (naira) rules
- Source for NGN screens: reference/poket-unified.html. Search for tVaNgn, tDepositMethod,
  ngnTempCreate, ngnIdCheck and VA_SPEC.NGN. Match its copy, states and behaviour exactly.
- Naira format: ₦ symbol, two decimals, thousands separators, followed by the grey code,
  e.g. "₦25,000.00 NGN". Amounts are integer kobo (cents), never floats. (Checkout's running text drops the code: see
  Checkout below.)
- One demo rate everywhere: ₦1,550 per $1 (DEMO_NGN_PER_USD in lib/money.ts), never the checkout prototype's 1/1540.
- Partner-neutral: never name a bank. Use the placeholder "[Partner bank]".
- Values not decided yet stay as visible placeholders: ₦[limit], ₦[minimum], [time], [handling], [hold], ₦[fee].
- Wallet only: NGN is funded only by bank transfer or conversion, never by card. (Paying a merchant with a naira card
  at Checkout isn't funding a balance.)
- Tier 0: NGN e-wallet allowed, but no dedicated account; funding uses a temporary account
  created per top-up, valid 30 minutes [time], for one exact amount.
- One naira balance: a user has one NGN balance, the NGN e-wallet (useNgn.ngnBalance). There's no merchant-held or
  other second naira balance, row or tag.
- The NGN balance screen (features/wallet/withdrawals/NgnBalanceScreen.tsx) has the reference's account card
  (vaCardHtml) between the actions and Transaction history, while virtual accounts are supported (NGN_VA.supported).
  Tapping it is useNgn.openAccountDetails:
  - No account, Tier 1 and up: "Get NGN account details" / "Receive NGN by NIP bank transfer. Free to receive.";
    opens the choice, then BVN or NIN, the ID check and terms.
  - No account, Tier 0: "Get NGN account details" / "Verify your identity to get account details"; opens identity
    verification, the wallet's step-up (StepUpIntro, stage 'verify') with the reference's reason "Account details
    are in your verified name, so we need to confirm who you are." Then the choice. It never opens a top-up:
    top-ups stay under Deposit (temporary account at Tier 0).
    - Demo stand-in: Continue raises the tier to 1 (useNgn.verifyContinue) in place of the verification steps
      (the reference's tStepupTier2 / tStepupTier3, Veriff), which aren't ported yet. When they are, Continue starts
      them and the tier changes only once verification succeeds; don't treat the instant upgrade as product
      behaviour. (The other step-ups, before a PIN or a conversion, just continue without changing the
      tier.)
  - An account: "NGN account details" with its status chip (Ready, Setting up, Frozen) and "Account number ••1234 ·
    [Partner bank]" (Setting up: "Usually ready within 1-2 business days"); opens its details.
  - Rejected: "NGN account details" with a "Not approved" chip (err), "We couldn't open your account. Try again or
    contact support." (NEW copy) and a "Try again" button (#vaRetry) that restarts the flow from the choice. The
    card itself doesn't open anything.
- Tier 1 and above: a dedicated NGN account needs BVN, NIN, NRBVN (or passport and residence
  permit, partner permitting), matched to the verified name and date of birth.
- Failed ID matches share Veriff's limit of 3 attempts. After 3, the account is locked for this step.
- Wallet only: credits from someone other than the account holder are held for review. (Checkout bank transfers don't
  check who sent them.)
- Keep the wallet's existing visual language. No new colours; use the wallet's tokens.

## Where NGN screens live
- Every NGN screen renders inside WalletShell's #panelBody, in the shadow root. One component per
  screen in features/wallet/ngn/screens/, registered in features/wallet/ngn/NgnBody.tsx.
- Screen switching and NGN state live in features/wallet/ngn/useNgn.ts; stages use the reference's
  v.stage names. Backend calls go through lib/mock-api.ts. Stages without a component show a
  "[Not ported yet]" placeholder. Preview one at /demo/wallet?ngn=<stage>.
- Format naira with formatNaira() from lib/money.ts; wrap the code in <span className="cur-code">.
- Use only the reference's class names (styles/wallet.css) and icons from lib/icons.ts. Tailwind
  classes don't apply inside the wallet. A missing icon is extracted from reference/poket-unified.html
  by script, verbatim (wallet engine icons go in WICON).
- `<Raw>` wraps HTML in a display:contents span, which breaks the wallet's direct-child selectors
  (`.settings-row>svg:last-child`, `.badge>svg`, `.qr-wrap>svg`). There, set the HTML on the parent
  element itself with dangerouslySetInnerHTML.

## Sending naira to a bank account
These rules apply to every NGN transfer to a bank account, whether to the user's own account or someone else's
(docs/decisions.md: withdrawing to your own bank is sending to yourself). "Payout" means only the Get paid widget;
don't use it for this in copy or new names. The current code still uses older names (features/wallet/payout/,
usePayout, createPayout, withdrawals.ts); read them as "send".
- Code: features/wallet/send/ chooses the recipient (useSend); features/wallet/payout/ runs the send from the amount
  on (usePayout.ts: amount, review, stepup, the checkout's PIN screens pin, pinLocked, pinResetOtp, moneyDone, then
  processing and done; ngn-account and ngn-bank for a new Nigerian bank account; payoutMath.ts: formatting and
  placeholders; screens/). WalletBody picks the open flow, else the wallet home.
- Send is the only way in: the wallet home and every balance screen have Send (no Transfer, Payout or Withdraw
  flows, and no separate recipient list or "Link a payout method" form). A balance screen passes itself as the source
  (useSend.open(source)). A saved recipient goes straight to the amount (usePayout.sendTo); "+ New recipient" → Bank account
  → NGN opens the bank-account step (usePayout.sendToNewBank). Back and "Change" return to Send.
- Previews: /demo/wallet?send=<stage>, a Send stage (recipients, new, unavailable...) or a later step of a send to
  Ngozi Eze (amount, review, pin, done...; ngn-account for a new Nigerian bank account).
- Every kind of send (any bank account, a debit card, a Poket user, a stablecoin wallet) uses the same amount,
  review, PIN, reservation and status screens (usePayout.executeSend; one summary in
  features/wallet/send/sendSummary.ts). The amount screen's title is "Send to {first name}" (your own accounts and
  wallets by their whole name), with Change. Review rows: To, Account holder (when the nickname differs), They get, You send and Rate (converting), Network (a coin: the wallet's, else the paying coin's), Fee
  (placeholders where undecided: ₦[fee] for Nigerian banks, [fee] for conversions involving NGN), Delivery, Total
  charged; then the first-payment notice for a new bank account. The button reads "Send {amount}" (what leaves
  the source).
  - Arrival: a first payment to any bank account is "After [hold]" and Scheduled; Nigerian banks "Usually within
    seconds"; US banks one speed, "1 to 3 business days", fee $[fee] (undecided, 0 in the maths; no Instant, Standard
    or Wire choice, no wire address); other banks "Within minutes"; debit
    cards "Instantly", fee $[fee] (undecided, 0 in the maths), status Sending; Poket users "Instantly" and straight
    to Paid; stablecoin wallets "About 1 minute", fee 1.50 in the coin.
  - Quick check (the exploration's): a first payment of $500 or more (USD value leaving the source) to a new debit
    card, stablecoin wallet or Poket user is held (status "review", Withdrawal.quickCheck) and reads "Held for a
    quick check" with "First payment to a new recipient. Usually under 15 minutes." on done and status. Bank
    accounts keep the first-payment hold instead; your own card isn't checked. A held Poket send is credited once
    it's paid.
  - A debit card in the verified user's name (any case or word order) is theirs: saved under Your accounts.
  - A new Nigerian bank account takes an optional nickname once the name is found; the enquiry's name stays the
    account holder.
  - Max is the whole balance less a decided fee (a wallet's); placeholder fees count as 0.
  - The review's total is what leaves the source, in the source's currency: the amount plus every decided fee (a
    wallet's 1.50 USDC paid from NGN shows as its naira), then " + ₦[fee]" (or $[fee], [fee] USDC) for an undecided
    one. It always matches what's reserved.
  - Poket users can be paid from card-funded USD. Their money keeps the FIAT-ONLY tag (shown on They get, review,
    done and the status screen; the mock API credits the matching tagged balance), so the rules on that money
    follow it.
  - Naira to Poket users (WGT-07): a recipient who can hold naira gets naira. One who can't (no BVN or NIN, or not
    eligible) gets USD, converted on receipt (targetFor in features/wallet/send/sources.ts): the amount screen says
    "{First name} can't hold naira. They'll get USD." with "They get ≈ $X USD" and the quote card (rate, fee as
    ₦[fee], They get; "Rate held for 0:30", then "Refresh rate"); review shows You send, They get and Rate.
    - The conversion is always naira to USD cash. It's never a stablecoin, so card-funded money never becomes one
      and the "NGN to stablecoins" block is never crossed. Other sources (USD, card-funded USD, USDC) arrive as they
      are; card-funded USD never becomes naira.
    - Mock recipients: Amara Okafor (@amara) can hold naira; Daniel Cho (@dcho) can't (lives abroad). Every other
      Poket user follows the demo bar's "Recipient can hold naira: Yes / No" (nairaRecipients.ts). The mock API
      refuses naira for a recipient who can't hold it (recipient_cannot_hold_naira).
    - Only the recipient's record is in USD. Your own record stays in the paying balance's currency (it shows in the
      NGN history and "on hold"), with what they receive as Withdrawal.targetKey. Your activity row and receipt lead
      with what left your balance (sentText): "To Daniel Cho", sub "₦5,000.00 NGN → $3.23 USD", amount "-₦5,000.00 NGN",
      and the receipt's amount ₦5,000.00 NGN with You send, They get, Rate and Fee. The done screen reads
      "₦5,000.00 NGN sent" with "{First name} gets $3.23 USD" underneath (the badge is the source's currency). This
      applies to every converted send. The recipient's activity (the wallet demo's "Recipient's activity"
      panel, listPoketActivity) shows what they received, in their currency, with its tag.
  - Debit cards (Send → New recipient → Debit card, the explore prototype's card fields): Visa and Mastercard debit
    only ("Only Visa and Mastercard debit cards."; a credit card shows "This is a credit card. Use a debit card." and
    the mock API refuses it). Only USD (open or card-funded) can pay a card; no conversion, no first-payment hold.
  - Reservation at the PIN: from NGN into "on hold" as below; from any other balance the total is taken out and given
    back if the send fails or is reversed (Withdrawal.reservedIn "source").
  - Stablecoin wallets need Tier 2 (saved or new), and the address is screened before review (the reference's
    "Checking this address"; a flagged address shows "We can't send to this address").
  - A Poket user starts on the NGN balance when opened from the home.
- Send's amount screen (features/wallet/send/SendAmountScreens.tsx): two boxes of the same design (Convert's From / To
  boxes): "You send" (label, "Available ..." and Max on top; the paying balance's chip and the amount below) and
  "They get" (label and the arrival on top, e.g. "Usually within seconds" or "After [hold]"; a fixed chip with the
  recipient's currency mark, name and any FIAT-ONLY tag, and the amount, "≈ " when converting).
  The keypad is hidden until You send's amount is tapped; "Done" at its top, or a tap anywhere else, hides it (on
  click, so the tapped control acts first), and Review then sits right under the amounts. The source defaults to the balance it was opened from if that can
  pay, else the recipient's currency. Another currency converts inline with the quote card below the boxes (rate and
  fee as ₦[fee] or the coin equivalent; They get is in its box, not repeated; the 30-second countdown and Refresh
  rate); it converts when the PIN is accepted.
  Review holds the same quote: the countdown shows there too, and on expiry Send is disabled until Refresh rate.
- A send that converts is one activity record (no separate conversion row): its row reads "To {name}" with
  "{source amount} → {target amount}" as the sub, and its receipt adds You send, They get and Rate, with one Fee row
  for both ("[fee] USDC + ₦[fee]").
- Send activity rows (WithdrawalRow, everywhere: the Activity tab, every balance's Transaction history, the on-hold
  list) are the reference's txRow: icon, "To {name}", a sub (the conversion, else the account), the amount that left
  the balance. A status chip follows the sub only while unfinished or gone wrong (Scheduled, Held for review, Held for
  a quick check, Sending, Failed, Reversed); paid rows have none, like the reference's completed rows.
- The chip never offers a balance that can't pay the recipient (features/wallet/send/sources.ts): card-funded money
  for naira or stablecoins, NGN for non-Nigerian bank accounts, and conversions
  Convert wouldn't allow.
- Copy: the button is "Send", processing is "Sending…", activity and status read "To {name}", and a reversal's
  notification is "Transfer reversed". Don't reintroduce "Payout" in this flow's copy.
- Sending is approved with the checkout's 4-digit PIN (tPin), not the reference's 6-digit passcode 'authorize' stage.
  Its copy is generic, for every kind of send: "Approves this payment.", "…to keep sending.", "Use it next time you
  send." Don't say withdraw or withdrawal there.
- Nigerian banks only: NGN can't pay a non-Nigerian bank account (the chip never offers it, sources.ts), so naira to
  a bank is never converted on the way. Opened from the naira balance, a recipient naira can't
  pay says why on the amount screen, with the paying balance's code (NEW copy): a bank outside Nigeria "Naira can
  only go to Nigerian banks. Paying from USD.", a debit card "Naira can't pay a card. Paying from USD."
- Name enquiry: a bank account is added on ngn-account (bank from NG_BANKS in lib/mock-api.ts, 10-digit number,
  automatic nameEnquiry()). A name that matches the verified user saves as their own account; any other name must be
  confirmed as a third-party payee. Without a successful name enquiry, an account can't be added.
- Every NGN e-wallet can send naira to Nigerian banks, within its tier limits (the step-up above ₦[step-up]); no
  balance or account type is excluded.
- Fees aren't decided: show ₦[fee] (FEE_PLACEHOLDER, fmtFee, fmtWithFee in payoutMath.ts) and keep them out of the maths
  (payoutFee returns 0 for NGN). Don't show the reference's computed naira fees.
- No speed choice: "Usually within seconds" (NGN_VA.arrives), on the amount screen's They get box; the fee, ₦[fee],
  is on review. (US banks have one speed too.)
- First-payment hold: the first NGN transfer to a bank account (recipient.paidBefore false) shows "First payment to a
  new account sends after [hold]" on amount and review (They get's arrival reads "After [hold]"), and its delivery is
  "After [hold]".
- Reservation: the amount (plus fee) is reserved from the balance when the PIN is accepted (reservePayout →
  ngnReserved), and stays reserved while the transfer is Scheduled, Held for review or Sending. Paid clears the
  reservation; Failed releases it back to the balance; Reversed (after Paid) returns the money and adds a notification.
  Rules: reservationEffect in features/wallet/payout/withdrawals.ts. Show reserved money as "₦X on hold" (the NGN balance
  screen: a grey line under the balance, like a balance's second line, "₦X NGN on hold ›"; the home NGN row's sub).
  It opens the list of sends on hold ("Sends on hold", the same activity rows).
- Statuses and chips (STATUS_META): Scheduled "Sends after [hold]", Held for review "We're checking this payment.
  We'll let you know.", Sending: the send's own arrival once it's sending (Withdrawal.eta: "Usually within seconds"
  for Nigerian banks, "About 1 minute" for wallets, "1 to 3 business days" for US banks...; never the naira copy for other sends),
  Paid, Failed / Reversed "Funds returned to your balance".
  Chips use the wallet's .va-status tones; no new colours.
- Step-up: a send whose USD value leaving the source balance (the amount plus every decided fee, in whatever
  currency it's paid from) is over ₦[step-up] shows the reference's step-up screen (tStepupIntro) before the PIN.
  It's never judged on what the recipient gets. The limit stays the ₦[step-up] placeholder on screen;
  STEP_UP_DEMO_USD_CENTS (₦20,000.00 at the demo rate, about $12.90) is a demo value only.

## Convert and the wallet home
- Convert lives in features/wallet/convert/ (useConvert.ts: the reference's S.cv stages; convertMath.ts: cv* helpers).
  Balances other than NGN are in features/wallet/useHoldings.ts; NGN stays in useNgn.ngnBalance. Preview at
  /demo/wallet?convert=<stage>.
- The wallet home is the reference's tWalletHome (features/wallet/home/): Stablecoins and Cash tabs read the holdings
  stores (useHoldings, useNgn). Under Cash, open NGN keeps its naira-format subtitle with "₦X on hold" and opens the
  NGN balance screen.
- Convert and naira (convertMath.ts):
  - Card-funded (FIAT-ONLY) money can't be converted to naira, as it can't to stablecoins: NGN is greyed out in
    "Convert to" with "Card-funded money can't be converted to naira" (tapping it: notice body "Choose another
    currency."), and the mock API refuses it. The tag still carries over on other cash-to-cash conversions.
  - A card-funded source gets no pre-selected target: To shows "Choose a currency" until the user picks one.
  - Opened with NGN as the target (Deposit NGN, the unavailable screen) and nothing that can convert into it: NGN
    stays the target, with "You have no USD or USDC to convert yet" and a Deposit button. Deposit isn't ported, so it
    shows the wallet's "Not in the prototype yet" notice (showNotPorted in useWalletToast.ts), like other unported actions.
  - Naira conversion fees are undecided: "Fee: ₦[fee]" (or "$[fee]", or the coin equivalent "[fee] USDC") and 0 in
    the maths. The quote card shows the rate, the fee, "You get" and the reference's 30-second countdown.
  - Naira conversions are capped at ₦[limit] per conversion ("Over your limit of ₦[limit] per conversion") and have a
    minimum, "Minimum is ₦[minimum]", both on the naira side. NGN_CONVERT_LIMIT_DEMO_KOBO and NGN_CONVERT_MIN_DEMO_KOBO
    are demo values that never show.
  - Before converting, the reference's limit and tier check runs (evaluateLimit in features/wallet/limits.ts, then
    step-up or "This exceeds Poket's limits"). Naira conversions show the tier limit as ₦[limit].
  - Demo "NGN to stablecoins: Blocked": naira defaults to USD; choosing USDC or USDT shows
    "Converting naira to stablecoins isn't available yet. You can convert to USD." with a button to switch to USD;
    the mock API refuses both.
  - Conversions from or to naira appear in the NGN balance history; their receipt opens from there and from
    "View transaction". The receipt is the reference's transaction detail (features/wallet/withdrawals/TxDetail.tsx):
    label icons, monospace right-aligned values, green bold "Completed", the date's " - " part in italics, and the
    Reference chip that copies. Stablecoin amounts there add " on {network}".

## Settings and instant transfers (WGT-09)
- Settings opens from the wallet header's gear (#openSettingsHeader): features/wallet/settings/. Settings, Security and
  Compliance are the reference's tSettings (with extSettingsRows), tSecurity and tAccountTier, with its class names
  (settings-list / settings-row, info-card, select-row). Rows: Compliance, Instant transfers, Payment methods,
  Subscriptions, Security, Log out; Payment methods, Subscriptions and Log out aren't ported (showNotPorted).
  Compliance leaves out the reference's demo-only "Reset demo spend history" and its admin compliance log.
- A settings row's HTML is set on the row itself so its chevron is the last <svg> (.settings-row>svg:last-child).
- Instant transfers (NEW copy, not in the reference): the Settings row reads "On · ₦X NGN a day" (the daily limit,
  formatNaira with the grey code) or "Off". Its screen has:
  - the switch "Instant transfers to banks" (the wallet's inline role="switch", as on the reference's dispute form)
    with "Send naira to Nigerian bank accounts in seconds, up to your daily limit. When it's off, you can't send to
    banks.";
  - "Daily limit": the limit (formatNaira), "Up to ₦[tier max] at your tier" and Change (disabled while off);
  - a raised limit waiting out the cooling-off period shows under it: the pending limit (formatNaira), "Starts in
    [cooling-off]" and "Cancel change". Demo bar on this screen: "Skip cooling-off" applies it now.
- Change opens the Daily limit screen: Send's amount box (cv-box: "Daily limit" and "Up to ₦[tier max] at your
  tier" on top, the NGN chip and the amount) with the keypad on demand (useKeypadOnDemand, as on Send's amount
  screen). Over the tier's maximum: "More than your tier allows (₦[tier max])." and Save is disabled (also when
  unchanged). A line says what Save will do (NEW copy): "A lower limit applies at once." / "A higher limit starts
  in [cooling-off]." Save asks for the PIN ("Lowers your daily limit." / "Raises your daily limit."): lower (or a
  lower change while a raise is pending) applies at once and replaces any pending raise; higher shows the toast
  "Your new limit starts in [cooling-off]" and becomes the pending limit. The typed amount survives Back from the
  PIN (useSettings.limitDraft). [cooling-off] is a placeholder: the period isn't decided.
  - the tier link "Raise your maximum: {next tier}" to Compliance (none at Tier 3).
- The tier maximums aren't decided: screens show "₦[tier max]". INSTANT_TIER_MAX_DEMO_KOBO (₦100,000 / ₦1,000,000 /
  ₦5,000,000 / ₦25,000,000 for Tiers 0 to 3) and the ₦50,000.00 default limit are demo values only; the limit in use
  is never above the tier's maximum (effectiveLimit).
- Demo bar "Instant transfers default: On / Off": what a new user starts with (switching starts the demo user over).
- Compliance's "Increase my limits" opens the step-up (no warning line: the user asked); Continue raises the tier by
  one, a demo stand-in for the verification steps, which aren't ported yet (as on the NGN account card).
- Turning instant transfers on asks for the PIN (the checkout's PIN screens, usePayout.approveWithPin: "Enter your
  PIN" / "Turns on instant transfers.", or create a PIN first), then the toast "Instant transfers are on." Back or
  a wrong PIN leaves them off. Turning them off needs no PIN: a confirmation sheet ("Turn off instant transfers?",
  "Sends to Nigerian banks won't go until you turn them back on.", Turn off / Keep them on; a tap outside cancels),
  then it takes effect at once. The sheet (.pk-sheet in styles/wallet-overrides.css) is new: the wallet has none.
- While off, a send to a Nigerian bank account (naira to a Nigerian bank, whatever pays it, USD converted too) shows
  "Instant transfers are off. Turn on" on the amount screen (#instantOffNotice), and Review is disabled (the store's
  toReview refuses too). "Turn on" asks for the PIN, then the send comes back as it was (approving or not).
  Poket users, other banks, cards, stablecoin wallets, Convert and Checkout aren't affected
  (blockedByInstantOff in features/wallet/settings/instant.ts).
- The daily limit in Send: while on, a send to a Nigerian bank can't take today's total over the daily limit (the
  limit in use: never above the tier's maximum). Today's total is the naira sent to Nigerian banks today, whatever
  paid it (getInstantSentToday in lib/mock-api.ts): scheduled, held and sending sends count; failed and reversed
  don't; Poket users, other banks, cards and wallets don't. Over it, the amount screen shows "Over your daily limit
  of ₦X. You have ₦Y left today." (formatNaira, grey code) with "Change limit" (NEW copy: it leaves the send for
  Settings → Instant transfers → Daily limit), and Review is disabled (the store's toReview refuses too). Exactly
  what's left fits (overDailyLimit in features/wallet/settings/instant.ts).
- Tests: e2e/settings.spec.ts (on and off, the PIN, the sheet, Send blocked when off, lowering, raising with
  cooling-off, cancel, skip, the tier cap, the daily limit in Send).

## Checkout (WGT-06: pay at checkout with NGN rails)
- Lives in features/checkout/: useCheckout.ts (flow, transfer and quote state), quote.ts (conversion quote and which
  methods can pay), screens/ (AddMethodScreen, AddCardScreen, BankTransferScreen, PayScreen, StatusScreens). Demo:
  /demo/ile-living. Checkout uses the prototype's classes (styles/prototype.css); the account card's va-* classes are
  copied into styles/overrides.css on the checkout's own colour tokens.
- Demo bar (components/demo/DemoBar.tsx, `checkout`): Order currency USD / NGN (the page's USD prices × ₦1,550), Merchant
  accepts (NGN card, NGN bank transfer, Conversion) and Bank transfer account (Dynamic / Merchant pool). The widget reads
  the merchant settings once, when it opens (useCheckout.settings). In the product CentryOS chooses Dynamic or pool;
  the demo bar stands in for that choice.
- Naira format at Checkout: titles, buttons, notes and the merchant page show naira without the code ("Pay ₦36,270.00
  to Ilé Living": formatOrder / formatNairaShort). Amount rows and cards keep the grey code (MoneyU: `.cur`).
- Rails offered (add-method) on an NGN order: only the naira rails the merchant accepts, first: "Card" (Nigerian debit
  or credit card) and "Bank transfer" (Pay into an account we show you). With Conversion on, the merchant's other rails
  follow (Card, Pay by Bank, Apple Pay, Google Pay, Cash App Pay), each marked with the chip "Paid in USD, converted".
  None accepted: "{merchant} can’t take payment for this order right now." A USD order keeps the usual list.
- Naira card: the same card form and validation (Verve supported), but number, expiry and CVC only, no ZIP. The saved
  method has currency "NGN". Dollar cards keep ZIP.
- Bank transfer shows an account card (Amount to send exactly, Account number, [Partner bank], Account name
  "{merchant} via Poket", Copy buttons) and "We’ll confirm as soon as it arrives". CentryOS matches each transfer to the
  order automatically (simulateCheckoutTransfer in lib/mock-api.ts). States: creating ("Getting your account"),
  waiting, arriving, paid (the done screen), underpaid / overpaid ("We received ₦X. [handling]" with Close), and, for
  the one-time account only, expired.
  - One-time ("Dynamic", createCheckoutVirtualAccount): one exact amount, a countdown from 30:00 [time]
    (NGN_TEMP_MINUTES), and the note "Use it once. A transfer for a different amount, or after it expires, is
    [handling]." Expired: "This account expired", "Don’t send to the old account number.", "Get a new account" (a new
    number and countdown). Demo actions: Transfer arrives, Less arrives, More arrives, Let it expire.
  - Merchant pool (getMerchantPoolAccount): the merchant's account, same card, no countdown and no "use it once" note.
    Demo actions: Exact amount arrives, Less arrives, More arrives.
  - Less / more in the demo is 10% under or over, in whole naira (demoTransferAmount).
- Paying in USD, converted (Conversion on, a dollar method on an NGN order): the pay screen shows the quote card before
  Pay: the rate ("1 USD ≈ 1,550.00 NGN") with the countdown ring, "Fee Covered by {merchant}" (the merchant covers
  conversion fees at Checkout), "You pay $X USD for ₦Y NGN", and "Rate held for 0:30". At 0 it reads "Rate expired.
  Refresh to get a new rate." and "Refresh rate" replaces Pay (a new quote moves up to ±0.2%); an expired rate can't
  pay. The dollars are rounded up to the cent. The summary's You pay, the button ("Pay $X"), a US bank's debit line
  ("…debit Chase checking ••• 6789 for $23.40 USD."), the processing screen and done ("You paid $X USD", Rate, Total
  ₦Y NGN) all use the quoted dollars.
- The wallet's naira conversion caps and checks (₦[limit], ₦[minimum], evaluateLimit) don't apply at Checkout.
- Conversion off on an NGN order: dollar methods on the pay screen are disabled (`.method.off`, aria-disabled) with
  "{merchant} only accepts naira for this order." and can't be selected; the first method that can pay is selected.
- Events to the merchant: payment.succeeded adds `rail` ("ngn_card", "ngn_bank_transfer"; dollar methods have none),
  `account_type` ("dynamic" or "pool", bank transfers) and, converted, `charged_amount`, `charged_currency`, `fx_rate`
  (naira per dollar). `amount` and `currency` stay the naira order. A short or over transfer sends
  payment.amount_mismatch with `order_reference`, `expected_amount`, `received_amount`, `rail` and `account_type`; its
  Close sends widget.closed with reason "amount_mismatch".
- Tier 0 rule: any payment under $500 needs no ID check, using the USD value of the naira total (orderUsdCents, rounded
  down); $500 or more verifies identity first (details, vendor, verifying, id-done; see docs/flows/checkout.md), then
  pays on the naira rails as usual.
- Tests: e2e/ngn-checkout.spec.ts.
